# WeBWorK problem written by Alex Jordan 
# Portland Community College
# ENDDESCRIPTION




##############################################

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
  "PGstandard.pl",
  "MathObjects.pl",
  "parserNumberWithUnits.pl",
  "PGML.pl",
);

##############################################
TEXT(beginproblem());


Context("Numeric");
$a = random(30,50,1);
$b = random(10,20,1)+$a;

$largeRate = NumberWithUnits("$a","hr");
$smallRate = NumberWithUnits("$b","hr");


$c = 1/(1/$a+1/$b);
$ans = NumberWithUnits("$c","hr");

##############################################

Context()->texStrings;

BEGIN_TEXT

Suppose that a large pump can empty a swimming pool in \($largeRate\)  and that a small pump can empty the same pool in \($smallRate\).  If both pumps are used at the same time, how long will it take to empty the pool?

$PAR

If both pumps are used at the same time, it will take \{ans_rule(15)\} to empty the pool.



END_TEXT

Context()->normalStrings;

##############################################
$showPartialCorrectAnswers = 1;


ANS( $ans->cmp(tolerance=>0.01));

$com = lcm($a,$b);
$comD = Formula("$com x");
$c = $com/$a;
$d = $com/$b;
$e = $c + $d;

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Let's understand how to use fractions to model work problems. Assume it takes a pipe [`5`] hours to empty a pool. If we treat the whole job as one whole, each hour, the pipe would empty [`\frac{1}{5}`] of the pool.

Similarly, if it takes a pipe [`x`] hours to empty a pool, each hour, the pipe would empty [`\frac{1}{x}`] of the pool.

Now we are ready to model this situation with math.

It's given that it takes the large pump [`[$a]`] hours to empty the pool. Each hour, the large pump empties [`\frac{1}{[$a]}`] of the pool.

Similarly, each hour, the smaller pump empties [`\frac{1}{[$b]}`] of the pool.

Assume that, if both pipes are turned on, it would take [`x`] hours to empty the pool. Thus, each hour, they can empty [`\frac{1}{x}`] of the pool.

Now we can write an equation and solve it:

    [``
\begin{aligned}
  &\frac{1}{[$a]}+\frac{1}{[$b]} = \frac{1}{x} \\
  &\frac{1}{[$a]} \cdot [$comD] +\frac{1}{[$b]} \cdot [$comD] = \frac{1}{x} \cdot [$comD] \\
  &[$c]x + [$d]x = [$com] \\
  &[$e]x = [$com] \\
  & \frac{[$e]x}{[$e]} = \frac{[$com]}{[$e]} \\
  & x \approx [$ans]
\end{aligned}
    ``]

In the second step, to get rid of all fractions, we multiplied each term with the common denominator [`[$comD]`].

By the solution we got, if both pipe are turned on, it would take [`[$ans]`] hours to empty the pool.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
